20.4 Results

Recommended order:

Open-data harmonization and coverage.
Score shift exists but is not the endpoint.
AF/LD predict distributional shift.
GWAS concordance labels reveal effect-size instability.
Mechanism features predict concordance.
Pathway-level risk is trait-specific.
HPRN-lite/full improves or clarifies mechanism attribution.
Local ancestry explains locus-level deviations in admixed groups.
20.5 Discussion
 This is a triage framework, not a clinical predictor.
 Score shift is not equivalent to accuracy loss.
 Mechanisms differ by trait/pathway.
 Some risk is statistically adjustable; some requires new diverse GWAS.
 Local ancestry may matter more than global ancestry for some loci.
 Limitations: summary-stat harmonization, no phenotypes, PGS selection bias, selection-stat noise.
